WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.170
<v Maya>Welcome to part seven.

2
00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:10.980
<v Maya>So far in lecture two we covered underwater weighing, DEXA, and the cardio tests, the Rockport walk and the YMCA step test.

3
00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:15.780
<v Maya>Today we do test four, muscular strength, and test five, muscular endurance.

4
00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:19.450
<v Dev>Strength first, endurance second, as the order says.

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.820
<v Maya>And remember why.

6
00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:25.810
<v Maya>Strength needs fresh muscles, and then endurance feels easier because of the priming effect.

7
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:32.230
<v Maya>Muscular strength is the ability of the working skeletal muscles to generate force in one maximum effort.

8
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:37.800
<v Maya>So the load must be the weight with which the person can do only one repetition, the one rep max.

9
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:42.560
<v Maya>There are two tests, bench press for the upper body and leg press for the lower body.

10
00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.930
<v Dev>And the score is the weight lifted?

11
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.050
<v Maya>Not quite.

12
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.360
<v Maya>The test measures relative strength, not absolute strength.

13
00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.290
<v Maya>Absolute strength is just the total weight lifted.

14
00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:57.760
<v Maya>Relative strength is the weight lifted in relation to the person's own body weight, a ratio.

15
00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:01.700
<v Maya>Relative strength equals weight lifted divided by body weight.

16
00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.980
<v Maya>It's also called the strength ratio.

17
00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:05.610
<v Dev>Why does that matter?

18
00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.890
<v Maya>A heavy person may lift more simply because they are bigger.

19
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.180
<v Maya>The ratio makes comparison fair.

20
00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:12.960
<v Maya>It comes out as a decimal.

21
00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:17.990
<v Maya>Then, using the client's gender, age and the ratio, you read the category from a chart.

22
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.520
<v Maya>The charts are percentile based, and there is a separate table for the leg press.

23
00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.050
<v Dev>Why the leg press, and not the squat?

24
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.510
<v Maya>The squat is unsupported, and it also engages the core.

25
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.100
<v Maya>Imagine two people tested with a squat.

26
00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.470
<v Maya>One has a strong core, one a weak core.

27
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.040
<v Maya>The weak core person may have good leg strength but cannot show it.

28
00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:44.190
<v Maya>The leg press is supported, like the bench press, so everyone gets a fair, supported test that doesn't depend on the core.

29
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.990
<v Dev>Walk me through a case.

30
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:55.160
<v Maya>The slide states the ratio directly, but to understand it, suppose, hypothetically, a man lifts one hundred and twenty four kilograms and weighs one hundred.

31
00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:59.240
<v Maya>One twenty four divided by one hundred is one point two four.

32
00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:00.880
<v Maya>He is thirty years old.

33
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:08.850
<v Maya>In the bench press chart for men, thirty to thirty nine years, one point two four is the ninetieth percentile, which is well above average.

34
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.790
<v Dev>How do I read that chart?

35
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:15.400
<v Maya>Rows are percentiles from ninety down to ten, grouped into five categories.

36
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:24.820
<v Maya>Ninety and eighty are well above average, seventy and sixty above average, fifty average, forty and thirty below average, twenty and ten well below average.

37
00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.650
<v Maya>Columns are age groups.

38
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:31.100
<v Maya>As age goes up the numbers go down, and women's numbers are lower than men's.

39
00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:36.740
<v Maya>For men thirty to thirty nine, the fiftieth percentile is zero point nine three, the average.

40
00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.430
<v Dev>And the leg press case?

41
00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.540
<v Maya>A thirty year old man presses twice his body weight.

42
00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:46.320
<v Maya>Hypothetically, two hundred divided by one hundred is two point zero zero.

43
00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.810
<v Maya>Remember it's a ratio of two to one, which is the number two, not one to two.

44
00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:59.190
<v Maya>In the leg press chart for men thirty to thirty nine, the eightieth percentile is one point nine three, and the ninetieth is two point zero seven.

45
00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:02.160
<v Maya>Two lies between them, so well above average.

46
00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:03.510
<v Dev>Pause and think.

47
00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:10.230
<v Dev>Is relative strength the weight lifted divided by body weight, or body weight divided by weight lifted?

48
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:11.650
<v Maya>Which one, Dev?

49
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.520
<v Dev>Weight lifted divided by body weight.

50
00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:15.450
<v Maya>Yes.

51
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:16.520
<v Maya>Beginners.

52
00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:19.130
<v Maya>Will this test be conducted for beginners?

53
00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:19.970
<v Maya>No.

54
00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:28.530
<v Maya>A beginner has no neural pathways formed yet, no form or technique, no kinetic chain, no Valsalva manoeuvre and no proprioception.

55
00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.480
<v Maya>So in the first test, skip it and do everything else.

56
00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:36.530
<v Maya>In the retest, test number two, treat it as the first time muscular strength is tested.

57
00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.190
<v Maya>From the third test, you can track progress.

58
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:45.690
<v Dev>One note, the question in the recording said muscular endurance, but the explanation was about strength.

59
00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:50.650
<v Maya>Right, and the E-book agrees that strength testing isn't prescribed for those starting out.

60
00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.210
<v Maya>Whether the rule also applies to endurance isn't stated.

61
00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:55.160
<v Maya>Recap.

62
00:03:55.210 --> 00:04:05.380
<v Maya>Strength is one rep max, ratio equals lifted divided by body weight, bench press and leg press, leg press instead of squat because it's supported, beginners skip at test one.

63
00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:06.540
<v Maya>Test five.

64
00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:13.620
<v Maya>Muscular endurance is the ability of the working skeletal muscles to contract for a long period without getting fatigued.

65
00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:18.080
<v Maya>Two tests, push-up for the upper body, curl-up for the abdominal region.

66
00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.550
<v Dev>Do we give a target number of reps?

67
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:21.390
<v Maya>No.

68
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.160
<v Maya>Both are done till failure.

69
00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:27.730
<v Maya>Endurance is tested by counting how many repetitions the client can do.

70
00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:35.010
<v Maya>The client is told the correct form and full range of motion, and the count is checked against the chart for that gender and age.

71
00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.390
<v Dev>What counts as a proper push-up?

72
00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.170
<v Maya>Full range of motion, as per the test makers.

73
00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:43.940
<v Maya>At the bottom, in the eccentric phase, the chin touches the floor.

74
00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:46.590
<v Maya>On the way up, the arms are fully extended.

75
00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.700
<v Maya>No half repetitions are counted.

76
00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:50.740
<v Maya>Tell the client before the test.

77
00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.250
<v Dev>And men and women do it differently?

78
00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:54.140
<v Maya>Yes.

79
00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:56.640
<v Maya>Males on the toes, palms and toes.

80
00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:59.280
<v Maya>Females on the knees, palms and knees.

81
00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:00.990
<v Maya>That isn't gender bias.

82
00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:06.720
<v Maya>The chart values were built keeping in mind genetic differences, and you must compare apples with apples.

83
00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:09.470
<v Maya>Even on the knees, the chin must touch the floor.

84
00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:15.360
<v Maya>A woman who can do the male version is top tier, but for the test the standard version must be followed.

85
00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:16.590
<v Dev>Cases?

86
00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:24.040
<v Maya>A thirty year old male doing thirty push-ups is excellent, because thirty or more is excellent for ages thirty to thirty nine.

87
00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:30.540
<v Maya>A thirty year old female doing twenty seven is also excellent, because twenty seven or more is excellent for her.

88
00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:33.780
<v Maya>The female numbers are lower for the same category.

89
00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:39.690
<v Dev>So the pattern is, the older, the lower the numbers, and the female thresholds are lower than male.

90
00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:40.830
<v Maya>Exactly.

91
00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:48.460
<v Maya>Your course notes flag a small printing oddity in the female sixty to sixty nine column, so check the printed table for that age group.

92
00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:50.420
<v Maya>The curl-up is a forward crunch.

93
00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:54.020
<v Maya>Spine flexion, the function of the rectus abdominis.

94
00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:57.540
<v Maya>In the crunch exercise, hands were interlocked behind the head.

95
00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.020
<v Maya>In the test, hands are not behind the head.

96
00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:03.020
<v Maya>The client lies supine, palms flat on the mat.

97
00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:04.880
<v Dev>Why palms on the mat?

98
00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:08.870
<v Maya>As the spine flexes, the palms travel to and fro along the mat.

99
00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:14.350
<v Maya>The distance the palm travels sets the range of motion for everyone, so results are comparable.

100
00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.780
<v Maya>Two pieces of tape are placed on the mat.

101
00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:21.880
<v Maya>Tape A is the starting position, and a second tape marks how far the fingertips must go.

102
00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:25.450
<v Maya>The fingertips touch the far tape and come back to tape A.

103
00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.100
<v Dev>How far apart?

104
00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.300
<v Maya>It depends on age, not gender.

105
00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:31.830
<v Maya>Younger than forty five, twelve centimetres.

106
00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:34.330
<v Maya>Older than forty five, eight centimetres.

107
00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:39.530
<v Maya>The distance is measured from the inside edge of one tape to the inside edge of the other.

108
00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:41.790
<v Maya>Each tape is two centimetres wide.

109
00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:46.930
<v Maya>For the younger client, a third tape goes four centimetres further than the eight, making twelve.

110
00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:51.970
<v Dev>So a seventeen year old uses twelve and a forty seven year old uses eight.

111
00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:52.960
<v Maya>Correct.

112
00:06:53.010 --> 00:07:00.600
<v Maya>Your notes say the age of exactly forty five isn't assigned, and the video once said younger than forty, so check your course material.

113
00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:02.260
<v Dev>What about cheating?

114
00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:04.720
<v Maya>Some people lift the palm to reach the tape.

115
00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:06.730
<v Maya>That repetition does not count.

116
00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.870
<v Maya>The whole hand stays grounded, going forward and back.

117
00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:11.280
<v Dev>And the speed?

118
00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.790
<v Maya>A metronome at forty beats per minute.

119
00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.180
<v Maya>Not ninety six, that would be too fast.

120
00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:19.420
<v Maya>And unlike the step test, here one beat is one phase.

121
00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:25.360
<v Maya>One beat for the concentric phase, one for the eccentric phase, so two beats make one repetition.

122
00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:29.060
<v Maya>Tick one, from tape A to the far tape, tick two, back to A.

123
00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:30.280
<v Dev>Cases?

124
00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:33.060
<v Maya>Male, thirty years, twenty five curl-ups.

125
00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:39.490
<v Maya>In the men's thirty to thirty nine column, the fortieth percentile is twenty six and the thirtieth is nineteen.

126
00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:42.920
<v Maya>Twenty five is just under twenty six, so below average.

127
00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:45.770
<v Maya>Female, thirty years, twenty five curl-ups.

128
00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:52.230
<v Maya>In the women's thirty to thirty nine column, the fiftieth percentile is twenty one and the sixtieth is twenty eight.

129
00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:55.030
<v Maya>Twenty five falls between, so average.

130
00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:57.710
<v Maya>Same number, better result for the female.

131
00:07:57.760 --> 00:07:59.070
<v Dev>Pause and think.

132
00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.420
<v Dev>How many beats for ten curl-ups?

133
00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:02.440
<v Maya>Twenty.

134
00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:03.950
<v Maya>Two beats each.

135
00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.230
<v Dev>That follows.

136
00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.800
<v Dev>Two times ten.

137
00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:08.190
<v Maya>One last note.

138
00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:16.800
<v Maya>The E-book describes a different partial curl-up test, with tapes ten centimetres apart, fifty beats per minute, one minute, and a maximum of twenty five.

139
00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:19.110
<v Maya>The lecture version is what we've taught here.

140
00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:20.280
<v Maya>Five things.

141
00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:25.670
<v Maya>One, strength is tested with one rep max, as a ratio of weight lifted to body weight.

142
00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:30.510
<v Maya>Two, bench press for the upper body, leg press for the lower body, because it's supported.

143
00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:37.300
<v Maya>Three, beginners skip strength at the first test, the second is their first strength test, and tracking begins at the third.

144
00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:44.090
<v Maya>Four, push-ups are done till failure, males on toes, females on knees, chin to the floor and arms fully extended.

145
00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:52.520
<v Maya>Five, curl-ups use tapes twelve centimetres under forty five and eight over, forty beats per minute, two beats per repetition, palms stay on the mat.

146
00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:55.660
<v Dev>Next, flexibility and the final quiz.

147
00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:56.930
<v Maya>See you in part eight.

